<!--
    kuna-guardedloop: a guarded do-while prints as a while loop
    (option guardedloop).

    Source (clang -O2 -fno-inline -fno-vectorize -fno-unroll-loops, x86-64):
      struct node *append(struct node *head, struct node *n)
        { struct node *t = head; if (!head) return n;
          while (t-&gt;next) t = t-&gt;next; t-&gt;next = n; return head; }
      void drain(struct st *s)
        { while (s-&gt;cur != s-&gt;end) s-&gt;cur = step(s, s-&gt;cur); step(s, s-&gt;end); }

    clang tests each loop condition once in front of the loop and again at
    its bottom.  The tests read memory, so looprotate leaves both loops.

    Stage: P8, ActionGuardedLoop on the final tree.  append's guard is the
    loop test on the value the assignment before the loop gives the walk
    pointer, so the loop prints as while (v1); the guard stays, as code
    after the loop runs only when it holds.  drain's guard is its loop test
    (a load of s-&gt;end on each side), the loop ends the guard's arm and the
    variables the guard reads are the loop's own, so the guard goes too.

    Pass 1 (default) prints two while loops and no do-while; pass 2
    (guardedloop off) the guards and the do-while loops.
-->
